  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES-East Nighttime Microphysics RGB imagery shows a \nwide variety of cloud structures, though KMQT radar returns have all \nbut entirely fallen off entirely. MRMS estimates of rainfall today \nhave been highly variable, with some locations along the state line \ngetting no measurable precip, but some locations as far north as \nnorthwest Delta County and southern Marquette County seeing between \n0.1 and 0.2 inches. The 500mb pattern currently consists of ridging \nover the south-central US and over the West Cost separated by a \ncutoff low over Wyoming with building ridging over the Canadian \nPrairie. A dry shortwave is noted over northern Ontario by RAP \nanalysis and water vapor imagery.\n\nToday, a cold front associated with the Ontario trough drops \nsoutheastward over Lake Superior and into the UP. Weak forcing and a \nlack of moisture will lead to spotty showers at most with the front \nitself. However, the surge of cooler air aloft will cause marginally-\nsupportive thermodynamic profiles for some lake-enhanced or lake-\neffect rainfall, with CAMs showing the orographic forcing of the \nwestern and north-central UP being enough to force weak \nprecipitation, though QPF will be low. This will also come with some \ngustier NE winds, with gusts of 20+ MPH forecast especially along \nthe Lake Superior shores. This in turn cranks wave heights up to 6 \nft within 5 miles of the Lake Superior shoreline, which will \ndrastically increase the risk of hazardous rip currents and \nchallenging swimming conditions for those trying to squeeze in a \nlate-September Sunday beach day at Lake Superior beaches. A Beach \nHazard Statement will be issued before sunrise today, lasting until \nsunset. Waves falling below 3 ft by Monday morning will cause the \nhazards to wane over at least Alger County, though uncertainty still \npersists in whether conditions will relax enough for Marquette \nCounty to not need another Beach Hazard Statement to kick off the \nwork week. Clearing skies overnight will allow temperatures tonight \nto fall to the 30s away from the shores of the Great Lakes. Patchy \nfrost is currently forecast as there is some uncertainty in the \ntiming of clearing and how effective radiational cooling will be. \nHowever, if more certainty is granted by the day's models, a Frost \nAdvisory may become necessary.\n\nIn the wake of the weekend disturbances, the ridging to the north of \nthe trough amplifies, causing split flow. This results in dominant \nsurface high pressure establishing over northern Ontario and Quebec \nthrough much of next week, likely bringing an extended period of dry \nweather to the UP. The latest NBM keeps us dry until Sunday but even \nthen, the precipitation signal is not particularly strong. Upstream \nridging aloft results in cooler continental flow aloft, and being on \nthe south/southwest periphery of a surface high results in cool \neasterly to northeasterly flow at the surface off of the Canadian \nShield. This brings cool conditions for the beginning of the week \nwith a gradual warming trend as the week goes on. For instance, the \nNBM even calls for up to 50% chances of sub-freezing temperatures \nfor Tuesday morning, though the LREF chances are significantly \nlower. By the end of the week, NBM highs are widespread 70+. The CPC \n6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks both show 50+% chances for the UP to be \nin above average temperatures for the end of September and a slight \nfavoring of below normal precipitation (30-40% chance).",
